into tls.he
lang he
use os, crypto.x509, crypto.ecc, crypto.hash, net.sock, net.tls

A standalone program that checks the TLS 1.3 server in `net.tls` against the library's own client, OpenSSL and curl, and prints one line per check. Everything named in this paragraph already exists in the imported library modules: this program must not define any of it, and defines only the helpers in the fenced block below and `main`. From `net.tls`, all in namespace `net`: the connection type `net::Tls`; `net::tls_open("127.0.0.1", port, protocol)` starts a client connection and `net::tls_accept(socket, certificate, key, protocol)` wraps an accepted socket as a server (the certificate is DER bytes, the key the raw private key); `net::tls_quic(server bool, host, protocol, parameters, certificate, key)` makes a connection without a socket whose handshake messages are moved by the helper `mem`; `net::tls_state(t)` is one of `net::TLS_HANDSHAKE`, `net::TLS_OPEN`, `net::TLS_CLOSED`, `net::TLS_ERR`. Fields of a `net::Tls` this program sets before the handshake: `want` (bytes: on a client, the SHA-256 the server certificate must have) and `group` (int: on a server, the only key exchange group it accepts). Fields it reads afterwards: `err` (bytes: the error text), `alert` (int), `hrr` (bool: the handshake went through a retry), `sel` (bytes: the agreed protocol), `qs` (a list of byte arrays: the traffic secrets, four after a full handshake), `ptp` (bytes: the peer's parameters). From `net.sock`: `net::listen(net::addr("127.0.0.1", 0))` gives a listening socket and `net::local_port(l)` its port. From `crypto`: `crypto::ec_generate(crypto::P256)` a fresh private key, `crypto::x509_self_signed(name, key, from int, days int)` gives DER bytes and a bool, `crypto::pkcs8_ec(key)` gives bytes and a bool, `crypto::sha256(bytes)` gives bytes. From os, without a namespace: `run_command(text) int`, `write_file(path, bytes) bool`, `read_file(path, out) bool` appends the file to a growable byte array, `time()` the unix time as a real, `sleep(seconds real)`, `args()` the command-line arguments with the program name first.

The helpers, exactly as written here. `sh` runs a command quietly and is true when it succeeds; `bg` starts a command in the background with its output in a file and creates the done file when it ends, after removing a done file left by an earlier run; `path` joins the work directory and a file name; `has` is true when the first bytes contain the second; `wait_file` waits up to some seconds for a file to exist; `acc` accepts the client's connection on the listener; `shake` runs both ends until neither is still in the handshake; `matrix` runs one client and server pair with the given suite and group, sends 1 MiB each way, closes, and is true when everything matched; `serve` accepts one outside client, reads one line (or one HTTP request), answers and closes, and returns what it read; `relay_flip` puts a relay between client and server that flips one bit in the client's last handshake record and is true when the server refuses it; `mem` moves handshake messages between two socketless connections, flipping one bit of the client's Finished message when asked; `fetch` starts `openssl s_server` and is true when the library's client reads its status page over the wanted TLS version; `client_cmd` and `curl_cmd` give the OpenSSL and curl command lines.

```he
sh(cmd const byte[:]) bool: run_command(str(cmd, " >/dev/null 2>&1")) == 0
bg(cmd const byte[:], out const byte[:], done const byte[:]): run_command(str("rm -f ", done, "; (", cmd, " > ", out, " 2>/dev/null; touch ", done, ") &"))
path(dir const byte[:], name const byte[:]) byte[>..]: str(dir, "/", name)
has(h const byte[:], n const byte[:]) bool:
	i ~= 0
	while i + n.len <= h.len:
		if h[i..i + n.len] == n: return true
		i += 1
	false
wait_file(p const byte[:], secs real) bool:
	t0 := time(); b byte[>..] ~= []
	while time() - t0 < secs:
		if read_file(p, b): return true
		sleep(0.01)
	false
acc(l int, c net::Tls&) int:
	fd ~= -1; t0 := time()
	while fd < 0 && time() - t0 < 5.0: net::tls_pump(c); fd = net::accept(l)
	fd
shake(c net::Tls&, s net::Tls&):
	t0 := time()
	while (net::tls_state(c) == net::TLS_HANDSHAKE || net::tls_state(s) == net::TLS_HANDSHAKE) && time() - t0 < 5.0: net::tls_pump(c); net::tls_pump(s)
matrix(l int, port int, cert const byte[:], key const byte[:], pin const byte[:], suite int, group int) bool:
	c ~= net::tls_open("127.0.0.1", port, "amni-v1"); c.only = suite; c.want.append(pin)
	if group == 0x17: c.group = group
	s ~= net::tls_accept(acc(l, c), cert, key, "amni-v1")
	if group == 0x18: s.group = group
	shake(c, s); msg byte[>..] ~= []; gc byte[>..] ~= []; gs byte[>..] ~= []; i ~= 0; j ~= 0; t0 := time()
	for x 1048576: msg.push(((x * 7 + x / 255) % 251) as! byte)
	while (gc.len < msg.len || gs.len < msg.len) && time() - t0 < 30.0 && net::tls_state(c) == net::TLS_OPEN && net::tls_state(s) == net::TLS_OPEN:
		if i < msg.len && c.wbuf.len < 65536: n := min(16000, msg.len - i); net::tls_write(c, msg[i..i + n]); i += n
		if j < msg.len && s.wbuf.len < 65536: n := min(16000, msg.len - j); net::tls_write(s, msg[j..j + n]); j += n
		net::tls_pump(c); net::tls_pump(s); net::tls_read(c, gc); net::tls_read(s, gs)
	open := net::tls_state(c) == net::TLS_OPEN && net::tls_state(s) == net::TLS_OPEN; net::tls_close(c); t1 := time()
	while net::tls_state(s) == net::TLS_OPEN && time() - t1 < 5.0: net::tls_pump(s)
	closed := net::tls_state(s) == net::TLS_CLOSED; net::tls_close(s)
	open && closed && c.hrr == (group == 0x18) && s.hrr == c.hrr && c.kx == group && s.kx == group && c.suite == suite && s.suite == suite && c.sel[..] == "amni-v1" && s.sel[..] == "amni-v1" && gc[..] == msg[..] && gs[..] == msg[..]
serve(l int, cert const byte[:], key const byte[:], alpn const byte[:], http bool, secs real) byte[>..]:
	got byte[>..] ~= []; t0 := time(); fd ~= -1
	while fd < 0 && time() - t0 < secs:
		fd = net::accept(l)
		if fd < 0: sleep(0.005)
	if fd < 0: return got
	s ~= net::tls_accept(fd, cert, key, alpn); done ~= false
	while !done && net::tls_state(s) != net::TLS_ERR && net::tls_state(s) != net::TLS_CLOSED && time() - t0 < secs:
		net::tls_pump(s); net::tls_read(s, got); n := got.len
		done = if http { n >= 4 && got[n - 4..] == "\r\n\r\n" } else { n > 0 && got[n - 1] == 10 }
		if !done: sleep(0.002)
	body := "hello from helium tls\n"; r := if http { str("HTTP/1.1 200 OK\r\nContent-Type: text/plain\r\nContent-Length: ", body.len, "\r\nConnection: close\r\n\r\n", body) } else { copy(got) }
	if done:
		net::tls_write(s, r[..]); k ~= 0
		while s.wbuf.len > 0 && k < 500: net::tls_pump(s); sleep(0.002); k += 1
	net::tls_close(s); got
relay_flip(l int, port int, cert const byte[:], key const byte[:], pin const byte[:]) bool:
	l2 := net::listen(net::addr("127.0.0.1", 0)); c ~= net::tls_open("127.0.0.1", net::local_port(l2), "amni-v1"); c.want.append(pin); a := acc(l2, c); b := net::connect(net::addr("127.0.0.1", port)); net::nodelay(a, true); net::nodelay(b, true); fd ~= -1; t0 := time()
	while fd < 0 && time() - t0 < 5.0: fd = net::accept(l)
	s ~= net::tls_accept(fd, cert, key, "amni-v1"); cb byte[>..] ~= []; sb byte[>..] ~= []; flipped ~= false
	while net::tls_state(s) == net::TLS_HANDSHAKE && time() - t0 < 5.0:
		net::tls_pump(c); net::recv(a, cb)
		while cb.len >= 5 && cb.len >= 5 + (cb[3] as int << 8 | cb[4] as int):
			n := 5 + (cb[3] as int << 8 | cb[4] as int)
			if cb[0] == 23 && !flipped: flipped = true; cb[n - 1] ^= 1
			net::send_all(b, cb[..n]); rest byte[>..] ~= []; rest.append(cb[n..]); cb = rest
		net::tls_pump(s); sb.clear(); net::recv(b, sb)
		if sb.len > 0: net::send_all(a, sb[..])
	t1 := time()
	while net::tls_state(c) == net::TLS_OPEN && time() - t1 < 2.0:
		sb.clear(); net::recv(b, sb)
		if sb.len > 0: net::send_all(a, sb[..])
		net::tls_pump(c)
	net::close(a); net::close(b); net::close(l2); flipped && s.err[..] == "bad record MAC" && s.alert == 20 && c.err[..] == "alert 20"
mem(c net::Tls&, s net::Tls&, flip bool):
	net::tls_start(c)
	for r 8:
		for lv 3:
			if c.qo[lv].len > 0:
				d ~= copy(c.qo[lv]); c.qo[lv].clear()
				if flip && lv == 1: d[d.len - 1] ^= 1
				net::tls_feed(s, lv, d[..])
		for lv 3:
			if s.qo[lv].len > 0:
				d ~= copy(s.qo[lv]); s.qo[lv].clear(); net::tls_feed(c, lv, d[..])
fetch(dir const byte[:], pin const byte[:], flag const byte[:], want12 bool) bool:
	pl := net::listen(net::addr("127.0.0.1", 0)); sp := net::local_port(pl); net::close(pl)
	bg(str("timeout 20 openssl s_server -accept ", sp, " -cert ", dir, "/cert.pem -key ", dir, "/key.pem -www ", flag, " -naccept 1"), path(dir, "ss.out"), path(dir, "ss.done"))
	page byte[>..] ~= []; v12 ~= false; tries ~= 0
	while tries < 70 && page.len == 0:
		c ~= net::tls_open("127.0.0.1", sp, ""); c.want.append(pin)
		if net::tls_wait(c, 3000) == net::TLS_OPEN:
			net::tls_write(c, "GET / HTTP/1.0\r\n\r\n"); t0 := time()
			while net::tls_state(c) == net::TLS_OPEN && time() - t0 < 4.0: net::tls_pump(c); net::tls_read(c, page); sleep(0.002)
			net::tls_read(c, page); v12 = c.v12
		else: sleep(0.1)
		net::tls_close(c); tries += 1
	wait_file(path(dir, "ss.done"), 3.0); has(page[..], "HTTP/1.0 200") && has(page[..], if want12 { "New, TLSv1.2" } else { "New, TLSv1.3" }) && v12 == want12
client_cmd(dir const byte[:], port int) byte[>..]: str("printf 'ping-amni\\n' | timeout 9 openssl s_client -connect 127.0.0.1:", port, " -tls1_3 -CAfile ", dir, "/cert.pem -verify_return_error -verify_hostname amni-lan -alpn amni-v1 -quiet")
curl_cmd(dir const byte[:], port int) byte[>..]: str("curl -s --http1.1 --cacert ", dir, "/cert.pem --resolve amni-lan:", port, ":127.0.0.1 https://amni-lan:", port, "/hello -m 7 -o ", dir, "/body.txt")
```

`main` does the following and prints the lines listed after `expect:` in that order. The work directory is the first command-line argument when there is one, otherwise "tls_test.tmp"; create it with `mkdir -p` through `sh`. Make a fresh key, a certificate for the name "amni-lan" valid from one minute before now for 3700 days, and the pin, which is the SHA-256 of the certificate. Write the certificate to "cert.der" and the key's PKCS#8 form to "key.der" in the work directory, then convert both through `sh` with `openssl x509 -inform der -in <dir>/cert.der -out <dir>/cert.pem` and `openssl pkey -inform der -in <dir>/key.der -out <dir>/key.pem`. Open one listener on 127.0.0.1 port 0 and take its port; every check below uses this listener, port, certificate, key and pin unless it says otherwise.
- For each suite in the order 0x1301, 0x1302, 0x1303 and inside that for each group in the order 0x1D, 0x17, 0x18: print "T3 ", the suite as the text "1301", "1302" or "1303", a space, the group as "x25519", "p256" or "p384 retry", a space, and the result of `matrix`.
- Start `client_cmd` with `bg`, output file "sc.out" and done file "sc.done" in the work directory; call `serve` with protocol "amni-v1", not HTTP, 9 seconds; wait up to 9 seconds for the done file; read "sc.out". Print "T3 openssl s_client " and whether both what `serve` returned and the file are exactly "ping-amni" followed by a newline.
- Start `curl_cmd` with `bg`, output file "curl.out" and done file "curl.done"; call `serve` with protocol "http/1.1", HTTP, 9 seconds; wait up to 9 seconds for the done file; read "body.txt". Print "T3 curl " and whether what `serve` returned contains "GET /hello HTTP/1.1" and the file is exactly "hello from helium tls" followed by a newline.
- Wrong pin: a client for protocol "amni-v1" whose `want` is the SHA-256 of the text "other"; the server is `net::tls_accept(acc(l, c), certificate, key, "amni-v1")`; then `shake`. Print "T3 control wrong pin " and whether the client is in the error state, its error text begins with "certificate rejected" and the server is not open.
- Wrong key: the same with the right pin on the client, but the server is given a second fresh key with the same certificate. Print "T3 control wrong key " and whether the client is in the error state, its error text is exactly "CertificateVerify failed" and the server is not open.
- Wrong protocol: the same with the right pin and key, but the client asks for protocol "other". Print "T3 control protocol " and whether the server's error text is exactly "no application protocol", its alert is 120 and the client's error text is exactly "alert 120".
- Print "T3 control flipped record " and the result of `relay_flip`.
- Message level: the client is `net::tls_quic(false, "amni-lan", "amni-v1", "client-params", "", "")` with `want` set to the pin, the server `net::tls_quic(true, "", "amni-v1", "server-params", certificate, key)`; call `mem` without flipping. Print "T3 message level " and whether both are open, neither went through a retry, each has four secrets and the four are equal pairwise, the client's peer parameters are "server-params", the server's are "client-params", and both agreed on "amni-v1".
- The same again with the server's `group` set to 0x18 before `mem`. Print "T3 message level retry " and whether all of the above holds except that both went through a retry.
- The same as the first message-level run but with flipping. Print "T3 control flipped finished " and whether the client is open, the server is in the error state, its error text is exactly "client Finished failed" and its alert is 51.
- The same as the first message-level run but the client asks for protocol "other". Print "T3 control protocol message level " and whether the server is in the error state, its error text is exactly "no application protocol" and its alert is 120.
- Print "T4 tls 1.3 " and the result of `fetch` with the flag "-tls1_3" and false, then "T4 tls 1.2 " and the result of `fetch` with the flag "-tls1_2" and true.
- At the very end, when no argument was given, remove the work directory with `rm -rf` through `sh`.

expect:
T3 1301 x25519 true
T3 1301 p256 true
T3 1301 p384 retry true
T3 1302 x25519 true
T3 1302 p256 true
T3 1302 p384 retry true
T3 1303 x25519 true
T3 1303 p256 true
T3 1303 p384 retry true
T3 openssl s_client true
T3 curl true
T3 control wrong pin true
T3 control wrong key true
T3 control protocol true
T3 control flipped record true
T3 message level true
T3 message level retry true
T3 control flipped finished true
T3 control protocol message level true
T4 tls 1.3 true
T4 tls 1.2 true
